Q: Why is the Foreground field being ignored in my WPF Button style? A: <p>The <code>ContentPresenter</code> creates an implicit <code>TextBlock</code> to render your string, and that <code>TextBlock</code> is likely picking up a theme style that overrides the <code>Button.Foreground</code> property. I'm pretty sure that's why your maroon setting is being ignored. Your other button works because the <code>Canvas</code> is binding directly to the property you want.</p>
<p>You can fix this by forcing the <code>ContentPresenter</code> to pass the button's foreground down via <code>TextElement.Foreground</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-xml prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;ContentPresenter x:Name=&quot;contentPresenter&quot;
                  TextElement.Foreground=&quot;{TemplateBinding Foreground}&quot;
                  ... /&gt;
</code></pre>
<p>This ensures any generated <code>TextBlock</code> inherits the button's color. You can find more detail on how these implicit styles interfere <a href="https://ikriv.com/dev/wpf/TextStyle/TextBlockStyle" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
